# ABSTRACT

This project focuses on the development of a Python-based Mining Health, Safety and Equipment Monitoring Application for KHWEZI MINING. The application is designed to centralise worker safety records, safety incidents, equipment information and maintenance data to support effective monitoring of mining operations. Its main features include role-based access control, worker health and safety monitoring, incident management, equipment condition monitoring, maintenance tracking and risk assessment. The system will use predefined thresholds to identify abnormal equipment temperatures and vibration levels, calculate risk scores, and generate alerts for conditions requiring attention. An interactive dashboard will display key performance indicators, including safety incidents, PPE compliance, equipment availability and critical alerts. Using Python, Pandas and Streamlit, the prototype aims to demonstrate how data analysis and visualisation can assist KHWEZI MINING in identifying potential hazards, prioritising maintenance and making informed operational decisions.

# 1. INTRODUCTION

KHWEZI MINING has identified the need to improve the monitoring of employee safety and mining equipment reliability. According to the company's statement in the project brief, its current monitoring processes rely on information collected from different sources, including safety observations, incident reports, equipment inspections, maintenance records and operator reports. The separation of this information makes it difficult for supervisors and mining engineers to obtain a consolidated view of workplace safety and equipment conditions.

To address this challenge, this project involves developing a Mining Health, Safety and Equipment Monitoring Application using Python. The application will provide a centralised platform for recording worker safety information, managing safety incidents, monitoring equipment conditions and tracking maintenance requirements. It will also incorporate risk assessment, automated alerts and an interactive dashboard to present important operational information.

The application will include role-based access for administrators, safety officers, mining engineers, maintenance engineers and managers. It will use data analysis to identify potentially unsafe conditions, flag equipment operating outside predefined monitoring limits and highlight maintenance requirements. The main objective is to demonstrate how programming and data visualisation can assist KHWEZI MINING in improving hazard identification, equipment monitoring and operational decision-making.

# 2. BACKGROUND

The project is based on KHWEZI MINING's requirement for a centralised system that integrates health, safety and equipment information. The application will monitor worker-related variables such as department, job role, shift, personal protective equipment (PPE) compliance, safety training status, fatigue level, safety observations, near misses and previous incidents. These records will help identify potentially unsafe working conditions and support the classification of workplace risks.

The system will also maintain a safety incident database containing information such as incident date, location, type, severity, injury status, cause, corrective action and incident status. Users will be able to record, search, filter and analyse incidents to identify trends. In addition, an equipment database will track haul trucks, loaders, excavators, drilling machines, bulldozers, crushers and conveyors using parameters such as operating hours, temperature, vibration, brake status, maintenance status, downtime and availability.

Equipment condition monitoring will apply the educational thresholds specified in the brief. For example, temperatures below 80°C will be classified as normal, while temperatures from 80°C to 100°C will trigger a warning. Vibration levels below 5 mm/s will be considered normal, levels from 5 mm/s to 8 mm/s will trigger a warning, and levels above 8 mm/s will be classified as critical. These values are prototype thresholds and must not be treated as actual manufacturer specifications or mine safety limits. The application will also calculate risk scores by multiplying likelihood by consequence on a scale of 1 to 5, and identify equipment requiring maintenance or experiencing excessive downtime.

Python will be used as the core programming language, with Pandas supporting data cleaning, filtering and analysis, and Streamlit providing the interactive interface. The dashboard will display indicators such as total safety incidents, near misses, PPE compliance, available equipment, equipment under maintenance, average equipment availability and critical alerts. These features will demonstrate how KHWEZI MINING can use centralised data and automated monitoring to support safety management, maintenance planning and operational decisions.
